  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This narrative review synthesizes research published since 2021 on the relationship between the built environment and subjective well-being, organizing the literature into five thematic categories: urban disruptions, mobility and accessibility, social and experiential dimensions, planning paradigms, and urban artificial intelligence. It maps how specific physical attributes—such as walkability, green spaces, housing quality, public transit investments, and traditional architectural styles—affect emotional and social well-being across different population groups.",
      "why": "It provides a consolidated framework that links modern planning paradigms like the 15-minute city and digital developments like urban AI directly to human happiness and social inclusion, helping planners justify well-being-focused interventions."
    }
  },
